{
  "script": {
    "name": "sample-aid-sc2-unit-generation",
    "wrapup_video_id": null,
    "login_required": false,
    "properties": {
      "generate_outline": "AI in Your Program\nRegular Unit  |  3 weeks  |  Required in all implementations and options\nUnit Overview\nGenerative AI is everywhere in students' lives, and this unit opens by getting inside how it actually works: students explore how generative AI systems produce text and images by predicting likely next pieces based on patterns in massive training data, building a working mental model they can reason from. With that foundation in place, students design and build block-based programs that incorporate a trained AI classification model as a functional component, making creative decisions about where the model fits, how the program should respond when the model is wrong, and whether the result actually serves the person it was built for. Throughout the unit, students work alongside a generative AI tutor that helps them plan, debug, and think through design decisions, and because they understand how generative AI produces its responses, they can reason about when to trust the tutor, when to push back, and when to ignore it entirely. Students might build an app that settles arguments, delivers a ruling on social situations, or predicts whether something they made would go viral. The creative challenge at the center of the unit is learning that incorporating AI into a program is a design choice with real consequences, and that the best designs keep the end user in view the whole way through.\nPurpose in the Course\n\n\n\nLearning Goals\nBy the end of this unit, students will be able to . . .\nExplain how generative AI systems produce text and image outputs through pattern-based prediction, including how training data shapes what the model can do and where it fails.\nDistinguish between classification models and generative AI models in terms of how they learn, what they produce, and where their outputs can be trusted.\nJustify whether a problem is best solved using procedural instructions, rule-based logic, or a data-informed model.\nPlan a block-based program using flowcharts or pseudocode that incorporates an AI classification model as a functional component.\nBuild a block-based program that uses conditionals and loops to control how the program responds to model outputs, including when the model is wrong.\nDirect a generative AI tutor with specific instructions and context, evaluating its responses based on conceptual understanding of how it produces them.\nRecognize the limitations of generative AI as a tutor, including hallucination, confidence calibration, and gaps in training data.\nEvaluate whether a program serves the people it was designed for, identifying where the AI model's outputs fall short and what would need to change.\nExplain how the design decisions students make when building AI-powered programs reflect the choices made by people who build AI-powered products in the real world, where those choices carry consequences for real users.\nStandards and Competencies\nJustify whether procedural, rule-based, or data-informed approach is best, and decompose accordingly\nMS-ALG-04 Justify whether a problem is best solved using procedural instructions, rule-based logic, data-informed methods, or a combination of these approaches.\nManage 1 Decide whether to use AI systems based on the nature of the task.\nManage 2 Choose an appropriate AI approach for a task by comparing how different AI systems operate and what they are best suited to do\nManage 3 Decompose a problem to determine when and how AI systems should be used to automate or augment tasks \nDesign and write a block-based program that incorporates an AI model\nMS-ALG-02 Model a given algorithm with a flowchart or pseudocode that includes a combination of control structures and procedures.\nEvaluate whether the program serves the people it is meant for\nShape AI 2 Evaluate AI systems using defined criteria, expected outcomes, and user feedback.\nMS-ALG-IM-10 Examine evidence of beneficial and harmful impacts, ethical issues, and biases of algorithms encountered in daily life \nMS-SOC-CE-44 Analyze how workers in different careers use computational thinking to solve real-world problems.\nUse and direct AI tools to support programming (introductory exposure)\nMS-ALG-05 Use an AI tool to generate outputs that assist in solving a computational problem .\nCreate with AI 3 Direct generative AI systems to elicit feedback, refine results and support reflection\nEngage with AI 3 Evaluate whether AI outputs should be accepted, revised or rejected \n\nStandards to Debate / Consider\nClassification-vs-generative distinction \nOur objective: Explain how generative AI systems produce text and image outputs through pattern-based prediction, including how training data shapes what the model can do and where it fails.\nAI Lit Framework:\nManage 2 Choose an appropriate AI approach for a task by comparing how different AI systems operate and what they are best suited to do\nAI Literacy Framework Knowledge Statements:\nThe Nature of AI 1.3 GenAI uses probabilities to generate advanced outputs across various modalities (e.g. text, audio, visuals) but lacks authentic human understanding and intent \nAI's Capabilities and Limitations 3.3 AI systems may produce different outputs in response to the same input, depending on both the input itself and how an AI system is designed to select or prioritise specific features and parameters \nUse and direct AI tools to support programming\nOur objectives: \nDirect a generative AI tutor with specific instructions and context, evaluating its responses based on conceptual understanding of how it produces them.\nRecognize the limitations of generative AI as a tutor, including hallucination, confidence calibration, and gaps in training data.\nAI Literacy Framework:\nEngage with AI 3 Evaluate whether AI outputs should be accepted, revised or rejected \nManage AI 4 Monitor and evaluate AI use throughout a problem-solving process \nReal-world product/consequences framing \nOur objective: Explain how the design decisions students make when building AI-powered programs reflect the choices made by people who build AI-powered products in the real world, where those choices carry consequences for real users.\nCSTA Standards\nMS-ALG-IM-10 Examine evidence of beneficial and harmful impacts, ethical issues, and biases of algorithms encountered in daily life \nMS-SOC-CE-44 Analyze how workers in different careers use computational thinking to solve real-world problems.\nMS-SOC-CE-45 Evaluate how automation in technology can create or replace jobs and change how people work.",
      "is_migrated": true
    },
